import . "sngl:internal/marks"

// Brackets a lifetime. `@mount` runs when the node enters the tree, `@unmount`
// when it leaves, and the pair is what a component uses to hold something the
// program has to give back -- a subscription, a scheduled callback, a request
// in flight.
//
// Ambient for the reason `context` is: the form that brackets a lifetime has to
// be available wherever a package declares state, and every tier above this one
// builds its own setup and teardown out of it. The mark is what the checker
// matches, so the name `effect` is shadowable like any other.
//
// It is placed, not declared: an effect is a node, so it sits where it belongs
// in the body and takes its lifetime from the tree around it. One inside an
// `if` lives as long as that branch does. A component places as many as it has
// things to hold, which is why this is a node rather than a hook on the
// declaration -- two brackets over different lifetimes cannot be one hook.
//
// `on` makes the bracket a keyed identity: while it holds the same value this
// is the same effect, and a different value is a different one, so the runtime
// ends the old lifetime and begins a new one -- `@unmount`, then `@mount`.
// Omitted, the bracket lives as long as the node does, which is what the
// parameter's default says: a key of a type with one value never changes.
//
// Each handler takes the value its own lifetime is keyed on. `@unmount` needs
// it because by the time teardown runs `on` already holds the next value, so a
// handler reading the cell would see what it is *not* tearing down; `@mount`
// takes it for symmetry, since the alternative is two handlers that differ for
// no reason a caller can see.
//
// **What a handler reads does not subscribe it.** A handler runs when its event
// fires, here as everywhere else in the language, and mount is an event. An
// effect that should run again when a value changes says so in `on`; nothing is
// inferred from the body. That is the whole difference from a computed, which is
// re-evaluated precisely because of what it reads.
#[builtin("effect")]
component effect<T = struct {}>(
    on T,
    @mount T,
    @unmount T,
) {}
